'''Soil and grain''' was a common political term in the [[East Asian cultural sphere|Sinosphere]] for the [[State (polity)|state]]. [[Shejitan]], the altars of soil and grain, were constructed alongside ancestral altars. Chinese monarchs of the [[Ming dynasty|Ming]] and [[Qing dynasty|Qing]] dynasties performed ceremonies of soil and grain to affirm their sovereignty at the [[Beijing Shejitan]], while Korean monarchs of the [[Joseon|Joseon dynasty]] did so at the [[Sajikdan|Seoul Sajikdan]].<ref>[https://books.google.com/books?id=8qvY8pxVxcwC&pg=PA371  A comparative study of thirty city states]</ref> It has also been rendered "gods of soil and grain" in English, owing to its associations of prayer and supernatural possibilities.<ref>[https://books.google.com/books?id=zqdHRAY4hAUC&pg=PA138 Hymes: Way and Byway (Google Books)]</ref> In Vietnam, corresponding soil and grain altars ({{lang|vi|đàn Xã Tắc}}) were established at historical capital of [[Hoa Lư]], [[Thăng Long]] (Hanoi) and [[Huế]].

During the Chinese [[Warring States period]], ministers defied their rulers by claiming a greater loyalty to the "soil and grain".<ref>[https://books.google.com/books?id=zhpLJgHZMTQC&pg=PA164 Envisioning eternal empire: Chinese political thought of the Warring States era]</ref>

A similar concept to ''sheji'' is that of the earth deities [[Tu Di Gong|Tudi]] and [[Houtu]].<ref>[[C.K. Yang (sociologist)|Yang, C. K.]] ''Religion in Chinese Society : A Study of Contemporary Social Functions of Religion and Some of Their Historical Factors'' (1967 [1961]).  Berkeley and Los Angeles: University of California Press. page 97</ref> It is also linked to {{Ill|Sheshen|zh|社_(宗教)}} or deities which are sometimes directly called soil (社)

