'''Shashoku''' (社稷) is the collective term for the '''sha''' (a ceremonial platform enshrining the tutelary deity of the land) and the '''jiki''' (a ceremonial platform enshrining the deity of grains).
Originally, these were the objects of ritual in the ceremonies of the feudal lords during the Zhou period; however, after the imperial period of the {{ill|Empire|ja|帝国|12=draft|12=draft}} of the Qin and Han dynasties, along with the {{ill|Tiantan|ja|天壇|12=draft|12=draft}}, {{ill|Ditian|ja|地壇|12=draft|12=draft}}, and {{ill|Zongmiao|ja|宗廟|12=draft|12=draft}}, the shashoku came to play a central role in the state rituals of China<ref name="ブリタニカ">[https://kotobank.jp/word/%E7%A4%BE%E7%A8%B7-75970]{{ill|Britannica International Encyclopedia|ja|ブリタニカ国際大百科事典 小項目事典|12=draft|12=draft}}</ref>, and by extension, it came to denote the very "【State】" or national polity.
It is related to the ancient spring and autumn equinox rituals, and its festival day, the {{ill|Shari|ja|社日|12=draft|12=draft}}, often coincides with the equinoctial period ({{ill|Higan|ja|彼岸|12=draft|12=draft}}) of spring and autumn. In Taiwan and Vietnam ({{ill|Vietnam|ja|越南|12=draft|12=draft}}), the term "sha" evolved from meaning the land deity to refer to administrative districts. In Japan, the original meaning of the word used to name a {{ill|Shinto Shrine|ja|神社|12=draft|12=draft}} is thought to have been a small shrine enshrining a tutelary land deity. From the notion of "sha" came the idea of a community association (a group of parishioners), which eventually led to the coining of modern words such as "kaisha" (company).
